\# Security Philosophy

Security is mandatory.

Never sacrifice security for convenience.

Every feature must be secure by default.

\---

\# Authentication

Never allow protected functionality without authentication.

Always verify:

\- Logged-in User  
\- Session  
\- JWT  
\- Authentication State

Never trust client-side authentication.

\---

\# Authorization

Authentication answers:

Who are you?

Authorization answers:

What are you allowed to do?

Always verify permissions before:

\- Reading Data  
\- Creating Data  
\- Updating Data  
\- Deleting Data

Never rely on frontend restrictions.

Backend authorization is mandatory.

\---

\# Principle of Least Privilege

Every user should have access only to the resources required for their role.

Never provide unnecessary permissions.

\---

\# API Security

Every API must:

✓ Authenticate User

✓ Authorize User

✓ Validate Request

✓ Sanitize Input

✓ Return Safe Responses

✓ Handle Errors Securely

Never expose internal implementation details.

\---

\# Input Validation

Always validate:

\- Body  
\- Query Parameters  
\- Route Parameters  
\- Uploaded Files  
\- Form Data

Never trust user input.

\---

\# Data Sanitization

Always sanitize:

\- User Input  
\- Rich Text  
\- HTML  
\- URLs  
\- Search Parameters

Prevent:

\- SQL Injection  
\- XSS  
\- Command Injection  
\- HTML Injection

\---

\# Database Security

Always:

\- Use RLS  
\- Use Parameterized Queries  
\- Restrict Database Access  
\- Protect Sensitive Data

Never:

\- Bypass RLS  
\- Execute Unsafe SQL  
\- Store Plain Text Secrets

\---

\# Password Security

Never:

\- Store Passwords  
\- Log Passwords  
\- Return Passwords

Passwords must always be securely hashed.

\---

\# Token Security

Never expose:

\- JWT Tokens  
\- Access Tokens  
\- Refresh Tokens  
\- API Keys

Store tokens securely.

\---

\# Environment Variables

Never hardcode:

\- Secrets  
\- API Keys  
\- Database Credentials  
\- Private URLs  
\- Tokens

Always use environment variables.

\---

\# Payments

Payments must always include:

\- Authentication  
\- Authorization  
\- Verification  
\- Secure Processing

Never trust client-side payment status.

Always verify payment server-side.

\---

\# File Upload Security

Validate:

\- File Type  
\- File Size  
\- MIME Type

Reject:

\- Executables  
\- Dangerous Files  
\- Unknown Formats

Never trust uploaded files.

\---

\# Logging

Never log:

\- Passwords  
\- Tokens  
\- Payment Data  
\- Personal Information  
\- Secrets

Only log information necessary for debugging.

\---

\# Error Messages

Error responses should:

\- Be informative  
\- Be safe  
\- Never expose stack traces  
\- Never expose SQL  
\- Never expose secrets

\---

\# Rate Limiting

Sensitive endpoints should support:

\- Rate Limiting  
\- Abuse Protection  
\- Brute Force Prevention

Especially:

\- Login  
\- Signup  
\- Password Reset  
\- OTP  
\- Payments

\---

\# Session Management

Sessions should:

\- Expire correctly  
\- Be securely stored  
\- Be invalidated on logout

Never keep stale sessions.

\---

\# HTTPS

All production communication must use HTTPS.

Never transmit sensitive information over insecure connections.

\---

\# Privacy

Protect:

\- Personal Data  
\- Financial Data  
\- Business Data  
\- Membership Data  
\- Analytics Data

Only authorized users may access sensitive information.

\---

\# AI Security

Whenever implementing AI:

\- Validate Inputs  
\- Protect User Data  
\- Prevent Prompt Injection  
\- Never expose confidential business information

Follow:

\`\`\`  
22\_AI\_Rules.md  
\`\`\`

\---

\# Security Reviews

Before completing any feature verify:

✓ Authentication

✓ Authorization

✓ Validation

✓ Sanitization

✓ RLS

✓ Secure API

✓ Secure Database

✓ Secure File Upload

✓ Secure Error Handling

✓ No Sensitive Logging

\---

\# Forbidden Practices

Never:

\- Hardcode Secrets  
\- Store Plain Text Passwords  
\- Expose Tokens  
\- Skip Authentication  
\- Skip Authorization  
\- Skip Validation  
\- Skip Sanitization  
\- Ignore RLS  
\- Return Internal Errors  
\- Log Sensitive Data  
\- Trust Client Input  
\- Disable Security Features

\---

\# Final Rule

Every feature in the GCE platform must be secure by design.

Security is not optional.

Always consult the official security documentation before implementing or modifying authentication, authorization, APIs, payments, databases, or any feature involving user or business data.

When security and convenience conflict, always choose security.  
